WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.667
CatBoost.

2
00:00:01.967 --> 00:00:06.600
Hand it raw categorical columns, and it handles the encoding safely for you.

3
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:13.333
Normally, you must encode categories yourself, with one-hot or hand-built target encoding.

4
00:00:13.633 --> 00:00:17.200
That means hundreds of columns, or a risk of leaking the label.

5
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:22.033
With CatBoost, you pass raw strings and simply name the categorical columns.

6
00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:24.833
The encoding happens inside, and safely.

7
00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:28.967
Here is the trick, called ordered target statistics.

8
00:00:29.267 --> 00:00:31.283
Put the rows in a random order.

9
00:00:31.283 --> 00:00:35.900
Each row's category is encoded using only the rows that came before it.

10
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:39.733
The first rows have no history, so they fall back to a prior.

11
00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:45.167
Row three, kepler, sees one earlier kepler with label one, so its encoding is one.

12
00:00:45.467 --> 00:00:47.488
No row ever sees its own label.

13
00:00:47.488 --> 00:00:49.967
That is exactly what prevents leakage.

14
00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:54.633
That is why it overfits less on high cardinality columns.

15
00:00:54.933 --> 00:00:59.433
Only the past is used, so a row's label never leaks into its own encoding.

16
00:00:59.733 --> 00:01:03.000
It also uses ordered boosting for extra resistance.

17
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.167
And symmetric trees, which keep the structure simple.

18
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:08.700
In code, it is short.

19
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.633
List the string columns: telescope, discovery facility, and spectral type.

20
00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:16.009
Set the iterations and learning rate.

21
00:01:16.009 --> 00:01:17.300
The defaults are great.

22
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.867
Then pass the list as cat features.

23
00:01:19.867 --> 00:01:22.133
No get dummies, no target encoding.

24
00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:26.700
Remember the three hundred facility column that broke one-hot?

25
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.867
CatBoost takes it as a raw string, and its ordered statistics handle rare facilities gracefully.

26
00:01:33.167 --> 00:01:39.133
The exoplanet pipeline loses forty lines of encoding code, and gains half a point of AUC.

27
00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:41.162
Let's recap.

28
00:01:41.162 --> 00:01:44.200
CatBoost eats categorical features natively.

29
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.667
Ordered statistics use only the past.

30
00:01:46.967 --> 00:01:49.000
So no leak, and no column blowup.

31
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.223
Pick it for tables full of string columns.

32
00:01:52.223 --> 00:01:53.267
Next up: S V M.
